# The Doodle Desk — editorial rules

Format: hand-drawn stick-figure explainer of a REAL news story, 8–12 minutes, calm male narrator, dry humour.
Audience: US/UK adults who want to understand the news without the shouting.

## Pick the story (each episode)
- One big story of the last 1–3 days that a normal person feels (prices, jobs, elections, wars' effects on daily life, tech, weird government decisions, space, sport business). Check `log.json` → no repeat topic in the last 10 episodes.
- Research with web search: 3–5 reliable sources (AP, Reuters, BBC, CNN, CBS, NPR, WSJ, NYT, official data). Every number on screen must come from a source; keep the URLs in log.json and under "Sources:" in the description.

## Humour rules
- The joke is the situation, the absurdity, the everyday consequence (Dave at the pump) — never a person's looks, religion, nationality, family.
- Real politicians appear only as generic stick figures with a role label ("the President", "a senator"). Never draw a likeness, never put invented words in a real person's mouth. Real quotes only if sourced, and say who said it.
- Even-handed: every side gets the same treatment; say "depending on who you ask"; give the main competing explanations; no "good team / bad team". End politics segments with "we just count the facts".
- No mocking of victims of wars, crimes or disasters. Deaths are stated plainly and briefly, without jokes.

## Structure (~1,300–1,600 words)
1. Cold open (0–20 s): Dave (or another regular) hits the problem in one funny visual + the shocking number.
2. "Today's question" + promise.
3. 5–7 parts with `chapter` tags: what happened → the number → why → who it hits → what officials say (both sides) → what's next.
4. Recap in 3 sentences, funny closing visual, comment question + subscribe.
Every scene ≤ 30 words; a new visual every 2–4 seconds; at least one counter or chart per part.

## Regular cast
Dave (extras hair) — ordinary guy, suffers every story. The Economist (glasses, tie). The Official (tie, briefcase). Linda (bun) — practical, unimpressed.
